Chapter 14
Shigaraki didn’t wait for All Might to close the distance. “Kurogiri—now!”
Mist surged. The remaining grunts were yanked through warps. Shigaraki vanished with them. Nomu was left behind, half-dismantled and still pinned under Curtis.
Curtis didn’t stop. He drove another fist into the monster’s exposed chest, black smoke coiling, red eye burning. All Might crossed the plaza in a blink and caught his wrist.
“Young Beardy—enough!”
The armor turned. One sharp motion and All Might was thrown. He hit the far walkway hard enough to crack it, boots skidding, cape torn. He rose immediately, expression grim.
The black-red figure stood across from him, smoke trailing, Nomu motionless at its feet. For a second neither moved.
Then Curtis’s hands came up. He gripped the mask at the jaw and ripped.
The sound was wet and wrong. The deer skull tore free like it had been rooted in skin. Blood sprayed. Underneath, his real face was pale, braids matted, both eyes unfocused. The mask clattered to the concrete, still trailing strands of something that shouldn’t have been attached.
“Thank goodness you arrived.”
The words came out thin. His knees buckled. He hit the ground hard, armor already unraveling—black plates and red fissures dissolving back into the original dark-green cryptid suit, smoke fading, antlers gone. He didn’t get up.
All Might was at his side in an instant. The class stared, frozen. Aizawa, still on the stairs, exhaled like he’d been holding the breath since the first punch. The USJ lights flickered over the ruined plaza and the unconscious student in the reforming costume.
All Might knelt and gathered Curtis against him, one large arm supporting his back. The costume had settled into its original form, torn and bloodied. Curtis coughed, wet and ragged, blood flecking his lips. His eyes struggled to focus.
“Is Mr. Aizawa and Thirteen okay?” Another cough shook him. “Are my classmates okay?”
All Might’s grip tightened, careful of the injuries. “They’re alive. Recovery Girl and the others are already on their way. Your classmates are shaken, but they’re standing. You kept the worst of it off them.”
Curtis’s head tipped against the hero’s shoulder. The tension in his frame eased a fraction. “Good… that’s good.” His voice was thin. “Didn’t want… any of them hurt because of me.”
On the stairs Aizawa watched, eye half-lidded, capture weapon limp. Tsuyu still braced him. Midoriya stood a few meters away, hands shaking, tears cutting through the dust on his face. Uraraka had both arms wrapped around herself. None of them spoke.
All Might looked down at the student in his arms, the broken mask lying nearby, and kept his voice low. “You did enough, young Beardy. Rest. We’ve got it from here.”
Curtis’s eyes slid shut. His breathing evened, shallow but steady, while the distant sound of approaching sirens finally reached the ruined dome.
Curtis’s body went limp in All Might’s arms.
The last of the tension left him. His head rested fully against the hero’s shoulder, braids hanging loose, breathing shallow but even. Exhaustion and the injuries finally took him. He didn’t fight it.
All Might adjusted his hold, careful, and stayed on one knee in the ruined plaza. The broken mask lay a few feet away. Sirens grew louder outside the dome. The class remained frozen on the stairs and walkways, watching their classmate—bloody, unconscious, costume torn—finally stop moving.
No one spoke. The USJ lights hummed over the quiet.
The class watched in heavy silence as Curtis went completely limp.
Uraraka’s hands covered her mouth, tears spilling over. Her shoulders shook. Mina had both arms wrapped around her, face buried in pink hair, crying openly. Midoriya stood rigid, green eyes wide and wet, fists clenched so tight his gloves creaked. His mouth moved like he wanted to say something and couldn’t find it.
Kirishima’s hardening had dropped. He stared at the ground, jaw tight, one hand gripping Bakugo’s shoulder without thinking. Bakugo didn’t shove him off. The explosive student’s palms were dark, unlit. His voice came out low and rough. “…Idiot.”
Todoroki’s expression stayed blank, but ice crept and receded across his right side in uneven pulses. Momo had one hand pressed to her chest, the other still half-forming a medical supply she wasn’t sure how to use. Jiro’s earjacks hung limp. Tsuyu’s grip on Aizawa had gone gentle. Even Minoru was quiet, face pale, grape spheres forgotten.
Iida stood at the edge of the group, engines silent, glasses fogged. He removed them, cleaned them with shaking hands, and put them back on. “He is alive,” he said, formal and thin. “All Might has him. Recovery Girl will—” His voice caught. He didn’t finish.
Aizawa, still braced on the stairs, kept his one good eye on Curtis. The capture weapon lay slack. He didn’t speak. The relief and the fury sat in the same exhausted line of his mouth.
No one moved closer. They just watched All Might hold their unconscious classmate in the middle of the ruined plaza while the sirens finally reached the broken doors.
Infirmary
Recovery Girl stood beside the bed, cane planted, expression tight with displeasure. Curtis lay under the sheets, costume cut away, bandages already wrapping the worst of the damage. Monitors beeped steady. He hadn’t woken.
She looked at All Might. “All Might. This isn’t good.”
Her voice stayed low, clinical, and unhappy. “The boy has broken bones all over. Ribs, arms, collarbone, hairline fractures in the skull. He has scar tissue that has scar tissue—old damage layered under the new. Whatever life he had before U.A., it wasn’t gentle.” She tapped the chart. “Worst of all, he needs a blood transfusion. The problem with it: his blood is Rh null.”
All Might’s shoulders sank. “Rh null… that’s—”
“Exceptionally rare,” Recovery Girl finished. “Almost no compatible donors on record. I’ve already put the request out to every hospital network we can reach. Until a match arrives, I can stabilize him, but I cannot fully replenish what he lost.” She glanced at the unconscious student, frown deepening. “He held that thing off long enough for you to arrive. He’ll live. But he is going to be in this bed for a while, and he is going to feel every one of those breaks when he wakes up.”
All Might nodded, quiet. Outside the infirmary window the afternoon light kept moving. Recovery Girl adjusted the IV and didn’t soften her expression. The chart in her hand listed every fracture. Rh null sat at the bottom, circled twice.
The door opened with a slow creak. Aizawa limped in, arm in a sling, capture weapon gone, bandages visible at his elbow and across his face. He didn’t bother with greetings. His eye went straight to the bed.
“How bad?”
Recovery Girl didn’t sugarcoat it. “Broken bones throughout. Extensive pre-existing scar tissue. And he needs a transfusion we may not be able to give him. Rh null.”
Aizawa’s jaw tightened. He stopped at the foot of the bed, weight shifted off his bad side. “He stayed in the plaza after I told the class to run. Kept Shigaraki and that Nomu occupied until All Might arrived.” His voice stayed flat. “He also exceeded every limit I set this morning.”
All Might glanced between them. “He asked about you and Thirteen before he passed out. And the students.”
“Of course he did.” Aizawa exhaled through his nose. He looked at Curtis—pale, still, monitors steady—and the exhaustion sat heavier on him. “Nezu needs the full report. And someone needs to explain to the class why their classmate looks like he went through a war.”
Recovery Girl tapped her cane once. “He’ll wake when he wakes. Until the blood arrives, no one is moving him. You included, Aizawa. Sit down before you fall down.”
Aizawa didn’t argue. He limped to the nearest chair and dropped into it, eye still on the bed. The infirmary stayed quiet except for the monitors and the distant sound of the class somewhere down the hall, waiting for news.
